For an air bubble rising in water, the no. of moles remain constant
\frac{P_{1}V_{1}}{T_{1}} = \frac{P_{2}V_{2}}{T_{2}}
Given:
• Initial volume \(V_1 = 2.9 \text{ cm}^3\)
• Depth \(h = 5 \text{ m}\)
• Initial temperature \(T_1 = 17 \text{ }^{\circ}\text{C} = 17 + 273 = 290 \text{ K}\)
• Final temperature \(T_2 = 27 \text{ }^{\circ}\text{C} = 27 + 273 = 300 \text{ K}\)
• Acceleration due to gravity \(g = 10 \text{ m/s}^2\)
• Density of water \(\rho = 10^3 \text{ kg/m}^3\)
• Atmospheric pressure \(P_{\text{atm}} = 10^5 \text{ Pa}\)
Initial pressure at the bottom of the pool:
P_1 = P_{\text{atm}} + \rho gh
P_1 = 10^5 \text{ Pa} + (10^3 \text{ kg/m}^3)(10 \text{ m/s}^2)(5 \text{ m})
P_1 = 10^5 \text{ Pa} + 5 \times 10^4 \text{ Pa}
P_1 = 1.5 \times 10^5 \text{ Pa}
Final pressure at the surface:
P_2 = P_{\text{atm}} = 10^5 \text{ Pa}
Using the combined gas law:
\frac{(1.5 \times 10^5 \text{ Pa})(2.9 \text{ cm}^3)}{290 \text{ K}} = \frac{(10^5 \text{ Pa})V_{2}}{300 \text{ K}}
Solving for \(V_2\):
V_{2} = \frac{(1.5 \times 10^5)(2.9)(300)}{(10^5)(290)}
V_{2} = \frac{(1.5)(2.9)(300)}{290}
V_{2} = \frac{1.5 \times 2.9 \times 30}{29}
V_{2} = 1.5 \times 0.1 \times 30
V_{2} = 4.5 \text{ cm}^3

### Detailed Solution


### Step 1: Calculate the initial and final areas of the loop.

Given:
• Radius of circular loop, \(r = 7 \text{ cm} = 7 \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}\)
• Magnetic field, \(B = 0.2 \text{ T}\)
• Time taken for conversion, \(dt = 0.5 \text{ s}\)
\text{Area of circular loop } A_{1} = \pi r^2 = \pi (7 \times 10^{-2})^2 = 49\pi \times 10^{-4} \text{ m}^2
The circumference of the circular loop is \(2\pi r = 2\pi (7 \times 10^{-2}) = 14\pi \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}\).
When the circular loop is converted into a square loop, its perimeter remains the same.
\text{Perimeter of square loop } = 14\pi \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}
\text{Side length of square loop } a = \frac{14\pi \times 10^{-2}}{4} = \frac{7\pi}{2} \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}
\text{Area of square loop } A_{2} = a^2 = \left(\frac{7\pi}{2} \times 10^{-2}\right)^2 = \frac{49\pi^2}{4} \times 10^{-4} \text{ m}^2

### Step 2: Calculate the change in magnetic flux (\(\Delta\phi\)).

The magnetic field is perpendicular to the plane of the loop, so the angle between the magnetic field and the area vector is 0 degrees. The magnetic flux is given by \(\phi = BA\).
The change in magnetic flux is \(\Delta\phi = B(A_{1} - A_{2})\). (Following the calculation order in the provided solution)
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \left(49\pi - \frac{49\pi^2}{4}\right) \times 10^{-4}
Using \(\pi \approx 3.14\):
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \left(49 \times 3.14 - \frac{49 \times (3.14)^2}{4}\right) \times 10^{-4}
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \left(153.86 - \frac{49 \times 9.8596}{4}\right) \times 10^{-4}
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \left(153.86 - 120.7801\right) \times 10^{-4}
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \times 33.0799 \times 10^{-4}
\Delta\phi \approx 6.616 \times 10^{-4} \text{ Wb}

### Step 3: Calculate the induced EMF.

The induced EMF is given by Faraday's law of induction:
\text{Induced EMF } |\varepsilon| = \left|\frac{d\phi}{dt}\right|
|\varepsilon| = \frac{6.616 \times 10^{-4}}{0.5} \text{ V}
|\varepsilon| = 13.232 \times 10^{-4} \text{ V}
Converting to milliVolts (mV):
|\varepsilon| = 13.232 \times 10^{-4} \times 10^3 \text{ mV}
|\varepsilon| = 1.3232 \text{ mV}
Rounding to two decimal places, the induced EMF is 1.32 mV.
The final answer is 1.32 mV.

According to the lens formula for magnification, the magnification (m) is given by:
m = \frac{f}{f + u}
When the size of the images are equal, it implies that the magnitudes of magnification are equal, but one might be inverted (real image) and the other upright (virtual image). Thus, we can write:
m_{1} = -m_{2}
Given the object positions are 8 cm and 24 cm. For a real object, u is negative. So, the object distances are \(u_{1} = -8\) cm and \(u_{2} = -24\) cm. Substituting these into the magnification formula:
\frac{f}{f + (-8)} = -\frac{f}{f + (-24)}
\frac{f}{f - 8} = -\frac{f}{f - 24}
Since \(f\) cannot be zero (it's a lens), we can cancel \(f\) from both sides:
\frac{1}{f - 8} = -\frac{1}{f - 24}
-(f - 24) = f - 8
-f + 24 = f - 8
24 + 8 = f + f
32 = 2f
f = \frac{32}{2}
f = 16 \text{ cm}
The focal length of the lens is 16 cm.

### Analysis of Motion

The problem states that the horizontal component of velocity for bead P at \(t=0\) is \(v\), and bead Q moves along the horizontal string AB with speed \(v\). For the purpose of comparing the time taken, we assume that the horizontal component of velocity remains constant at \(v\) for both beads throughout their respective motions towards point B.
The time taken to cover a certain horizontal displacement \(X\) with a constant horizontal velocity \(v\) is given by \(t = X/v\).
For bead Q, it starts at point A and moves along the horizontal string AB to point B. Its horizontal displacement is the length of the segment AB, let's denote it as \(X_{Q}\).
For bead P, it starts at point S on the semi-circular path and moves to point B. Its horizontal displacement is the horizontal distance from S to B, let's denote it as \(X_{P}\).
From the provided diagram, it is visually evident that the horizontal distance from A to B (for bead Q) is greater than the horizontal distance from S to B (for bead P).
X_{Q} > X_{P}
Since the horizontal component of velocity \(v\) is the same for both beads, we can write their respective times as:
t_{P} = \frac{X_{P}}{v}
t_{Q} = \frac{X_{Q}}{v}
Comparing these two expressions, because \(X_{Q} > X_{P}\) and \(v\) is a positive constant:
t_{Q} > t_{P}
Therefore, the relation between \(t_{P}\) and \(t_{Q}\) is \(t_{P} < t_{Q}\).

